    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "okay that makes sense. so you're saying the salt basically makes every hash unique even if the passwords are the same. can you explain how a rainbow table actually works in this scenario? like how does the salt specifically break the table's effectiveness?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "A rainbow table is a massive lookup table that pre-computes the hash of millions of possible passwords to speed up cracking those with no salt. When a salt is added, the input to the hashing function becomes unique for every user, meaning a password \"Password123\" hash differently for \"UserA\" than it would for \"UserB,\" completely breaking the attacker's ability to use the pre-computed table. Consequently, the attacker can no longer look up a single password hash in a single table entry and must perform a slow, individual calculation on each password hash.",
