    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "got it. so the salt makes the attacker have to start from scratch for every single user instead of using one big list. is there any other way they might try to bypass this, or is salting pretty much the gold standard for preventing those kinds of pre-computed attacks?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Salting is indeed a critical component, but it is rarely used alone; it must be combined with slow hashing algorithms like bcrypt, scrypt, or Argon2 to effectively prevent memory or hardware-accelerated attacks. Attackers might also try \"memory attacks\" where they use GPU or ASIC hardware to try billions of passwords per second, which brute force salting can only mitigate by increasing the size of the salt or using iterative algorithms. Furthermore, if you already have the adversary's database in plaintext, this threat is moot. Ultimately, combining a unique key-based salt with a computationally expensive hashing function remains the gold standard for password security in modern systems before moving to authenticated encryption.",
